replies.ghazal|none|full:
Before I knew the letters, I sat for hours turning the page;
the pictures were a country, and my hunger was burning the page.

My mother's finger walked beneath each word the way a lamp walks a hall;
I wanted what she had, that easy calm, discerning the page.

The shapes refused me: *b* was *d*, the *m* a stammering bridge;
I cried, and blamed the alphabet for overturning the page.

Then on a cereal box one morning a word stood up and spoke;
since then the signs, the walls, the buses keep returning the page.

Now I read the rain, the timetables, the faces of strangers in line;
still something in me misses the dark before learning the page.

Slow Reader, you were never slow: you were only in love,
and love's long work is this, by inches, earning the page.

*Radif: "the page." Qafia: -urning / -earning (turning, burning, discerning, overturning, returning, learning, earning).*

replies.haiku|frankenstein|full:
Snow on the hovel —
through a chink, Felix reading;
sounds turn into signs

Spring, and the stranger —
Safie's lessons in the dusk;
I learn them as well

August wood, moonlight:
a leather case of three books;
Adam's face, not mine

replies.ghazal|frankenstein|full:
I learned my letters at a stolen lesson, without a name, my maker;
I read of Adam's happy making, and it was not the same, my maker.

Felix read the *Ruins of Empires* to the Arabian by the fire;
I learned that men are ranked by birth and gold, and I had neither claim, my maker.

Through a chink in the hovel wall the sounds became their signs;
the paper spoke the cottage's own words, and I crept nearer to the flame, my maker.

In August, in the wood, I found three books in a leathern case:
Plutarch for virtue, Werter for tears, and Milton for the blame, my maker.

Then in the pocket of thy coat I found thy journal of four months;
each line recoiled from what it made. I read, and learned my shame, my maker.

Wretch, thou hast read thyself in water, and in moonlight, and in his hand;
there is no word in any book for thee. Then give me one: a name, my maker.

*Radif: "my maker." Qafia: -ame (name, same, claim, flame, blame, shame, name).*

replies.ghazal|frankenstein|lexical:
Before I had a single letter, through the chink I set my gaze, I read;
the faces of the cottagers, their sorrow, and the hearth's low blaze, I read.

When Felix bent above the page for Agatha and the old man,
he made the sounds he made in speech: so, signs for speech, in those slow days, I read.

A girl went by with a pail of milk, patient and sad, her hair in plaits;
I had no word for her, nor any word for kindness; only, in her ways, I read.

Plutarch raised me from my wretched sphere to love the heroes of the past;
of Numa, Solon, and Lycurgus, peaceable law-givers' praise, I read.

In Milton, Adam came forth guarded, perfect, from a loving hand;
in Satan's gall and envy, in his every bitter phrase, I read.

Hideous scholar: Who was I? Whence did I come? What was my end?
No page replied; my questions back, through every book's long maze, I read.

*Radif: "I read." Qafia: -aze / -ays (gaze, blaze, days, ways, praise, phrase, maze).*

replies.ghazal|frankenstein|agentic:
A leathern case lay in the August wood; I made my place in these books.
I bore them to the hovel, and I searched for my own face in these books.

Werter taught me gloom, and spoke of death in ways I did not understand;
I wept for his extinction, and could not say why sorrow had such grace in these books.

Plutarch raised me from my wretched sphere to love the heroes of old;
the peaceable law-givers, Numa and Solon, ran their gentle race in these books.

And Milton: God at war with what He made; and Adam, made and blest;
he spoke with angels, he was guarded; there was love's embrace in these books.

But I was wretched, helpless, and alone, and like no man I read of;
Satan, with the gall of envy rising, was my only trace in these books.

Creature, thou didst read it all as true history, and wept;
there were gods and men and devils, and for thee, no space in these books.

*Radif: "in these books." Qafia: -ace (place, face, grace, race, embrace, trace, space).*

replies.ghazal|phaedrus|full:
You wish to learn to read? Then sit beneath the plane, and read, my friend;
but tell me: is that Lysias beneath your cloak alive, or dead, my friend?

The cicadas over us were men once, who forgot to eat for song;
they watch to see if we will talk at noon, or nod the head, my friend.

Theuth carried letters to the king, a specific for the memory;
the king said: not for memory, but reminding; men will seem wise instead, my friend.

A writing is like a painting: it has the attitude of life;
ask it why, and it repeats the one thing it has always said, my friend.

The husbandman in earnest sows in fitting soil and waits eight months;
the garden of Adonis greens in eight days, and in eight days its green is shed, my friend.

Socrates, you who never wrote a line, be honest with yourself:
the only book you read is a living soul, in which the word is bred, my friend.

*Radif: "my friend." Qafia: -ead (read, dead, head, instead, said, shed, bred).*

replies.ghazal|phaedrus|lexical:
"Read me Lysias's beginning, Phaedrus, every line again,
that I may have his exact words, and weigh them, his and mine, again."

You read: "You know how matters stand with me..." and stop. Did he say first what love is?
He did not define it; he began at the end. Read me his first design again.

A man who reads of purges and of vomits in a book calls himself physician;
ask him to whom, and when, and how much: he can only point to the page, and its sign, again.

Say "iron," say "silver," and every mind holds the same thing;
say "justice," "goodness," and we part company, and drink a different wine again.

The old man sows a garden of letters against the forgetfulness of age;
but the word graven in the learner's soul can speak for itself, and grow divine again.

Socrates, you spoke in ecstasy, and cannot well remember what you said;
you too must beg the boy to read you back, and make the old words shine again.

*Radif: "again." Qafia: -ine (line, mine, design, sign, wine, divine, shine).*

replies.ghazal|phaedrus|agentic:
Theuth came to Thamus with his letters: here, he said, is a gain to remember.
The king replied: they will trust the marks outside, and strive in vain to remember.

You, father of letters, love your children, and cannot judge their use;
they will seem omniscient and know nothing; what they hear will not remain to remember.

Ask a painting what it means: it keeps a solemn silence;
ask a written speech, and it gives back one unvarying refrain to remember.

At Dodona the men of old heard truth from an oak, and it was enough;
you ask who the speaker is, and from what country, before the truth is plain to remember.

In the garden of letters the old man sows for his amusement
memorials against forgetfulness: a pastime, and a chain to remember.

Socrates, you left no writing; you walked out past the walls and talked;
and those who loved you wrote you down: your living word was slain, to remember.

*Radif: "to remember." Qafia: -ain (gain, vain, remain, refrain, plain, chain, slain).*

replies.ghazal|analects|full:
At fifteen, the Master said, he set his heart to learn;
at seventy he crossed no line: it took a life, the art to learn.

Learn without thinking, and you stand there dazed among the strips;
think without learning, and you are in danger. Keep both, not one apart, to learn.

Hui listened all day long and never questioned, and seemed slow;
then the Master watched him on his own, and saw him play his part, to learn.

In the Grand Ancestor's temple he asked about everything, and they laughed;
asking, he said, is the rite: the ignorant man's best start to learn.

Like bone filed, like jade carved: Zigong heard what was not said;
the poem is a map, and he read beyond the chart to learn.

Little Learner, say you know what you know, and say you don't know what you don't;
then if you hear the Way at dawn, at dusk you may depart, to learn.

*Radif: "to learn." Qafia: -art (heart, art, apart, part, start, chart, depart).*

replies.ghazal|analects|lexical:
Is it not a pleasure to practice what you've learned, in time,
and friends who come from far away, when at last the road has turned, in time?

Be filial at home, respectful out of doors, trustworthy in words;
and with the strength that's left, the arts: so the order is discerned, in time.

Zixia said: serve your parents fully, keep your faith with friends,
and though you claim no learning, the name of learned is earned, in time.

The North Star keeps its place; the other stars pay court around it;
three hundred poems never swerve: to that one phrase the reader has returned, in time.

At fifteen he set his heart on learning; at seventy, crossed no line;
between them, every season, doubt and Heaven's will slowly burned, in time.

Border Guard, you met him once and said the world would hear him like a bell;
a wooden tongue in bronze: the world, which had not listened, would be concerned, in time.

*Radif: "in time." Qafia: -earned / -urned (learned, turned, discerned, earned, returned, burned, concerned).*

replies.ghazal|analects|agentic:
Before the books, be filial at home; abroad, respect your elders, be polite, the Master said;
with strength still left, then turn to the arts, as dawn turns into light, the Master said.

One clever in his words and pleasing in his face is short of the humane;
the one who loves learning does not stuff himself, nor fret about his roof at night, the Master said.

A man of position without integrity will not persevere in learning;
do not fear your mistakes; stay close to those who do their best, and set your errors right, the Master said.

Zigong: poor and not fawning, rich and not proud? That is all right, he answered;
better, poor and joyful, rich and loving the rites: the jade carved bright, the Master said.

Do not worry that others do not know you; worry that you do not know them;
the North Star stands, and the other stars revolve and pay it court in sight, the Master said.

Little Scholar, at fifteen he set his heart on learning, and at seventy
he followed what his heart desired and did not overstep. Begin tonight, the Master said.

*Radif: "the Master said." Qafia: -ight (polite, light, night, right, bright, sight, tonight).*
